From ab459b300e6369d1c6077d9fc7674ed0810cb9eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 12:42:51 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Real shadows from the sun and the moon under the new look

Trees, buildings, fences, carts, creatures and heroes now cast real
shadows onto the ground, the buildings and each other: long and cool at
dawn and golden hour, short at noon, dappled under the leaves, and faint
from the moon at night. 1.12 had only its baked ground shade and a blob
under a unit; both give way as the real shadow comes in.

Our own depth pass, since Bevy's DirectionalLight shadows reach only its
PBR shaders and never the retained static pass: the visible sun (or the
white moon) on the lighting sun's own bearing, its elevation held to at
least 10 degrees; the view out to 120 yd (low) or 180 yd (high) cut into
two or four slices, each a camera-rotation-proof sphere snapped to its
texel grid so edges never swim; one Depth16 layer a slice of a 2048
square map, drawn before the main pass from the static world's own
regions (leaves cut by their texture), every model mesh in the world
(skinned from the shared palette) and the terrain, culled per slice with
small props left out of the far ones. Terrain, model and static-world
shaders read it with textureLoad (no sampler), PCF on low and PCSS-lite
on high, and take away only the sun's share of a pixel's light, never
the ambient, with a little cool fill at golden hour. Interiors take none.

worldShadows 0 off, 1 low (default), 2 high; lookshadow <strength>
[distance] | grade holds them for film; worldLook 0 is 1.12 untouched.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
